# Summary
Introduced: the new control-plane Unix RPC transport contains unchecked nested length fields in request deserialization. The outer frame payload is bounded, but collection capacities are derived from untrusted counts rather than from the remaining payload size or configured limits.
The commit adds a Unix-domain control-plane RPC server. The frame reader caps the total frame payload at 8 MiB, but the payload decoder then reads internal length/count fields directly from attacker-controlled bytes. For RefreshNodeHeartbeat, read_node_heartbeat reads observation_count and immediately calls Vec::with_capacity(observation_count). A client that can connect to the private control-plane socket can send a small, checksum-valid frame with observation_count set to a very large u32, without including corresponding observation records. The allocation happens before trailing/truncation validation or node authorization, so the request can force an enormous allocation attempt and may abort or exhaust memory in the control-plane worker/process. The CRC64 frame checksum does not prevent this because the client can compute a valid checksum for the malicious payload.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the introduced Unix control-plane path accepts/deserializes client-controlled RPC frames before request-specific authorization.
- [x] Confirm the frame layer bounds only the outer payload and that the checksum is client-computable, not an authenticity protection.
- [x] Confirm RefreshNodeHeartbeat deserialization reads a nested observation_count via PayloadReader::read_len without checking remaining payload or a maximum.
- [x] Confirm allocation occurs immediately from the attacker-controlled count before truncation validation and before the authority refresh method is called.
- [x] Dynamically validate with a small checksum-valid frame that triggers an oversized allocation/crash; attempt valgrind and debugger corroboration.
## Report
Validated the suspected unchecked nested length allocation in Rust. Built a targeted PoC crate at /workspace/argmin/validation-poc using the real public storage::control_plane::handle_control_plane_unix_stream path over UnixStream::pair. The PoC sends a checksum-valid control-plane RPC frame with a 36-byte RefreshNodeHeartbeat payload and observation_count=4294967295. Code evidence: crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:1997-2009 bounds only the outer payload length before reading it; crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:2016-2018 verifies a client-computable checksum; crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:1878-1881 dispatches RefreshNodeHeartbeat to read_node_heartbeat before calling the authority; crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:2105-2106 reads PG observations length and immediately calls Vec::with_capacity(observation_count); crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:2404-2407 returns a raw u32 length as usize without checking remaining payload or a protocol maximum. A sanity run with count=1 returned only Err(RpcProtocol { message: "truncated control-plane RPC payload" }), showing the PoC reaches the decoder. The crashing run with POC_RLIMIT_AS=200000000 aborted with exit 134 and "memory allocation of 137438953440 bytes failed". LLDB confirmed the crash chain: alloc::vec::Vec::with_capacity(capacity=4294967295) -> storage::control_plane::read_node_heartbeat at control_plane.rs:2106 -> build_control_plane_unix_response at control_plane.rs:1880 -> handle_control_plane_unix_stream at control_plane.rs:1833 -> PoC main. Valgrind was attempted but is not installed in the container.

